.TH RLING 1 "2026-08-29" "rling 1.86" "Password Recovery"
.SH NAME
rling \- remove, intersect, deduplicate and analyse line-oriented lists
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rling
.RI [ options ]
.I input output
.RI [ removefile " ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rling
reads
.IR input ,
removes every line that also appears in any
.IR removefile ,
and writes what is left to
.IR output .
Duplicate lines within
.I input
are removed as well, by default.  With
.B \-c
the sense is inverted and the lines
.I common
to input and remove files are written instead.
.PP
It is the tool for the routine set arithmetic of a password campaign:
subtracting an already-tried wordlist, intersecting a candidate list with a
known-cracked list, deduplicating a concatenation of a dozen leaks.  It is
substantially faster than the
.BR sort (1)
and
.BR uniq (1)
pipeline it replaces, and it preserves input order unless told otherwise.
.PP
The names
.B stdin
and
.B stdout
may be used in place of any filename.  Both
.I input
and
.I output
are required, even when no remove file is given \(em that form is a pure
deduplication.
.PP
With
.B \-q
.B rling
performs frequency analysis on the input rather than set arithmetic, and any
additional files are matched against it.
.SH "PROCESSING MODES"
.B rling
offers four strategies with different memory and speed characteristics.
They are mutually exclusive; the last one named wins, and a change is
announced on standard error.
.TP
.B "hash (default)"
In-memory hash table.  Fastest, highest memory use.
.TP
.B \-b
Binary search instead of a hash table.  Slower, but noticeably less memory.
.TP
.B \-f
File-backed binary search.  Slower still, smallest resident footprint.
Combine with
.B \-M
and
.BR \-T .
.TP
.B \-2
rli2 mode.  All files must already be sorted.  Lowest memory use of all,
and the only mode in which
.B \-M
has any effect.
.PP
.B \-s
makes
.B \-b
and
.B \-f
substantially faster.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-d
Remove duplicate lines from the input.  On by default.
.TP
.B \-n
Do
.I not
remove duplicate lines from the input.
.TP
.BI \-D " file"
Write the removed duplicates to
.IR file .
The same set of lines is written on every run, but their order varies
between runs unless
.B \-t 1
is used.
.TP
.B \-c
Write the lines common to input and remove files, rather than the
difference.
.TP
.B \-s
Sort the output.  The default is input order.
.TP
.B \-i
Ignore missing or unreadable files on the remove list instead of failing.
.TP
.BI \-l " length"
Restrict all matching to lines of exactly
.I length
bytes.
.TP
.BI \-t " count"
Worker thread count, 1 to 32768.
.TP
.BI \-p " prime"
Force the hash table size.
.TP
.BI \-M " size"
Maximum memory, with an optional
.BR K ,
.B M
or
.B G
suffix.  Only effective in
.B \-2
mode; used in any other mode it reports the setting and then warns that it
will do nothing.
.TP
.BI \-T " path"
Directory for temporary files, used by
.BR \-f .
.TP
.BI \-q " flags"
Frequency analysis on the input.
.I flags
is any combination of:
.RS
.TP 5
.B a
all of the below
.TP
.B c
count
.TP
.B l
length
.TP
.B w
word
.TP
.B s
running statistics
.TP
.B h
append histogram
.RE
.IP
At least one valid flag is required.
.TP
.B \-v
Debug output.
.TP
.B \-h, \-?
Print the usage summary and exit.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 on normal completion, 1 on a usage error, a missing file without
.BR \-i ,
or an allocation failure.  A total runtime line is printed to standard
error on the way out.
.SH EXAMPLES
Deduplicate a concatenation, preserving first-seen order:
.PP
.RS
.nf
cat leak*.txt | rling stdin merged.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Subtract everything already tried:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rling candidates.txt fresh.txt tried1.txt tried2.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Keep only the candidates that are known to have cracked something:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rling \-c candidates.txt useful.txt cracked.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Deduplicate a list too large for memory, using pre-sorted inputs:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rling \-2 \-M 4G \-T /var/tmp huge.sorted out.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Length and word-frequency profile of a wordlist:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rling \-q clw words.txt stdout
.fi
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR mdxfind (1),
.BR dedupe (1),
.BR splitlen (1),
.BR getpass (1),
.BR procrule (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Waffle.
